Build a Professional AI-Powered Notes Studio in My Existing Data Engineering Platform

Objective

Upgrade my existing Data Engineering Practice Platform with a premium, colorful, AI-powered Notes & Study Material module.

Use the provided design reference as the visual direction. The final UI should look like a professional learning platform with dark navy backgrounds, electric blue accents, colorful subject cards, clean typography, structured educational notes, code examples, diagrams and a responsive mobile layout.

IMPORTANT: Do not rebuild the project from scratch. Inspect the existing application and integrate the feature without breaking existing functionality, routes, authentication, SQL lab, Python lab, PySpark lab, Azure & Databricks, ETL Testing, Interview, AI Interview or Progress & Analytics.

1. Sidebar and Navigation

Add a new sidebar item named "Notes & Study Material".

Maintain the existing navigation structure and design language.

The Notes module should have these pages:

- Notes Dashboard
- My Notes
- PDF Library
- AI Notes Generator
- Bookmarks
- Revision Notes
- Recently Viewed
- Create Note
- Note Details / Reading View

2. Notes Dashboard Design

Build a responsive dashboard with:

- Header: Notes & Study Material.
- Global search for notes, chapters and concepts.
- Colorful subject cards with icons and note counts.
- Recently viewed and bookmarked notes.
- Featured notes.
- Reading progress.
- Upload PDF button.
- Generate with AI button.
- Create Note button.
- Difficulty filters and technology filters.
- Empty states for new users.

Subjects:
SQL, Python, PySpark, Azure Data Factory, Databricks, ETL & Data Warehousing, Data Engineering System Design, Data Engineering Concepts and Interview Preparation.

Each subject card should open its corresponding organized notes library.

Do not hardcode fake statistics. Populate counts and progress using actual saved records.

3. PDF Upload and AI Notes Generation

Allow authorized users to upload study PDFs.

The workflow must be:

1. Upload PDF.
2. Validate the file.
3. Extract digital PDF text.
4. Use OCR when a PDF contains scanned pages.
5. Detect chapters, headings, subheadings, code blocks, tables and important concepts.
6. Split long documents into manageable sections.
7. Generate structured notes using a configured AI provider.
8. Render the generated content in the Notes Editor.
9. Allow the user to preview, edit and approve the notes.
10. Save the notes and source PDF.
11. Provide source page references wherever available.

Create real processing states:
Uploading, Extracting Text, Organizing Topics, Generating Notes, Saving and Completed.

Handle failures, retries, large PDFs, duplicate uploads and partial processing. Never display fabricated progress or pretend that AI processing succeeded.

If AI or OCR credentials are unavailable, explain the missing configuration and provide setup instructions.

4. Colorful Notes Reading Interface

This is the most important component.

Create a premium reading experience with three areas on desktop:

LEFT SIDEBAR:

- Table of contents.
- Chapters and subtopics.
- Expandable sections.
- Active section indicator.
- Bookmarks.
- Navigation between topics.

CENTER CONTENT:

- Topic title and breadcrumbs.
- Short introduction.
- Clearly structured headings.
- Concise but sufficiently detailed explanations.
- Colorful concept cards.
- Definition boxes.
- Important points.
- Warning and common-mistake boxes.
- Code examples with syntax highlighting.
- Copy-code buttons.
- Expected output.
- Comparison tables.
- Flowcharts and educational diagrams.
- Related topics and practice links.

RIGHT SIDEBAR:

- Quick revision.
- Key takeaways.
- Important definitions.
- Interview tips.
- Reading progress.
- Related labs.
- Bookmark controls.

On mobile, convert sidebars into drawers or collapsible panels and show the main note content without horizontal overflow.

5. Automatic Colorful Formatting

Automatically format AI-generated notes using a consistent design system.

Use:

- Blue for core concepts.
- Purple for deeper explanations.
- Green for examples and correct approaches.
- Amber for important points.
- Red for warnings and common mistakes.
- Cyan for interview tips and shortcuts.

Use these colors as restrained accents with readable backgrounds and sufficient contrast. Avoid excessive gradients, oversized cards and decorative clutter.

Support light mode and dark mode while preserving the same content hierarchy.

6. Adaptive Notes Length

Notes must be medium-length by default: neither excessively long nor too short.

Use this adaptive structure:

1. Topic overview.
2. Core concepts.
3. Detailed explanations.
4. Important points.
5. Practical examples.
6. Syntax and code snippets, where relevant.
7. Diagrams and comparison tables, where useful.
8. Common mistakes.
9. Interview questions and answers.
10. Quick revision summary.
11. Practice questions.

Adjust sections according to the actual source material. Do not force every section into every note.

Preserve technical accuracy. Do not invent information or misrepresent additional AI explanations as content from the uploaded PDF. Keep source-derived content distinguishable from supplementary explanations.

7. Example: PySpark Notes

Create an example note structure for "PySpark DataFrame Transformations".

Include:

- Overview of DataFrames.
- select(), filter(), withColumn() and groupBy().
- Explanation of transformations versus actions.
- Short code examples with syntax highlighting.
- Sample input and output tables.
- A visual execution flow where appropriate.
- Common mistakes.
- Interview questions.
- Quick revision cards.
- Link to the existing PySpark Lab.

The example is for demonstrating the interface. Do not treat demonstration content as real saved user notes unless it is explicitly stored as sample content.

8. Example: SQL Notes

For SQL topics, support:

- Query examples.
- Formatted SQL code blocks.
- Sample tables and expected results.
- Joins, CTEs, window functions and aggregations.
- Query explanation.
- Common interview traps.
- Related SQL Lab practice.

Provide a "Try in SQL Lab" action only if the existing lab can safely accept the selected query.

9. Notes Organization and Management

Implement the hierarchy:

Subject → Chapter → Topic → Notes → Sections.

Allow users to:

- Create folders and subfolders.
- Rename and edit notes.
- Add personal annotations.
- Bookmark individual notes and sections.
- Mark sections for revision.
- Search across note titles and content.
- Filter by subject, difficulty and source PDF.
- Sort by recently updated, recently viewed and alphabetical order.
- Duplicate and delete notes.
- Export notes to PDF.
- Print clean study notes.
- Resume reading from the last saved position.

Save edits and reading progress persistently.

10. PDF Library and Original Document Viewer

Create a PDF Library that stores uploaded documents.

Provide:

- PDF title and source filename.
- Subject and chapter association.
- Upload date.
- Processing status.
- Generated-notes status.
- Original PDF preview.
- Page navigation and zoom.
- Open generated notes.
- Retry failed processing.
- Delete and replace document actions.

Where supported, clicking a source citation in the generated notes should open the relevant original PDF page.

11. Database and Backend Integration

Inspect the existing codebase and database before implementing.

Reuse the existing authentication, external Supabase configuration, storage and backend services if present.

Create or update suitable structures for:

- Notes folders.
- Notes.
- Note sections.
- Uploaded documents.
- Processing jobs and statuses.
- Bookmarks.
- Personal annotations.
- Reading progress.

Use migrations that preserve existing data. Implement appropriate Row Level Security, ownership checks, storage policies and upload validation.

Keep AI provider keys and privileged credentials on the server. Do not expose secrets in frontend code.

Use background processing for long documents where appropriate. Ensure retries are safe and prevent accidental duplicate records.

12. Functional Requirements

Every control must work:

- Search and filters.
- PDF upload.
- Generate notes.
- Preview and edit.
- Save and reopen.
- Bookmarks.
- Table-of-contents navigation.
- Copy code.
- Related lab navigation.
- Export and print.
- Reading progress.
- Error handling and retries.

Use real application data instead of hardcoded dashboard statistics.

13. Final Implementation Workflow

First inspect the existing project architecture, current routes, components, database schema and storage configuration.

Then provide a short implementation plan identifying what must be reused, added or modified.

Implement the feature incrementally. Verify the complete PDF-to-notes workflow, data persistence, access permissions, mobile responsiveness and existing lab functionality.

Deliver a working Notes Studio, not just a static UI mockup.

Keep the final design visually close to the supplied reference while following the existing application's architecture and conventions.
